{"id":"u-0fad56a2-18a8-476c-b8f9-100c0fea6614","src":"community","title":"FBEOD A/B","author":"Diezel Training","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/QufPT2-fbeod-ab","desc":"Train 3–4× per week (Mon A / Wed B / Fri A / Sun B) or 3× per week with a rolling pattern (A/B/A → B/A/B). Almost every muscle group is trained three to four times per week with enough recovery to keep session quality high.\n\nThe structure is modular: exercise slots are movement patterns, not fixed lifts. Swap variations based on equipment, joint comfort, and preference — as long as you stay close to failure and progress the first working set.\n\nHOW TO PROGRESS\n• Set 1: double progression toward the top of the rep range at 0–2 RIR\n• Sets 2+: AMRAP at 0–1 RIR — let fatigue dictate rep count\n• Weight gate: hit the top of the rep range with pristine form → repeat that weight next session before adding load\n• Alternative: add 2.5 kg/month on compounds when reps are solid or the weight feels easy\n\nWARM-UP (every session)\n1. General — raise core temperature (incline walk, bike, jump rope)\n2. Specific — 1–3 ramp-up sets before your first working set on each exercise\n3. Scale volume to the lift: more warm-up sets for heavy compounds, fewer for isolation\n\nKEY PRINCIPLES\n• Rest as long as needed — especially 2–5 min on compounds\n• Rep ranges are flexible; effort near failure matters most\n• Re-order exercises for your goals — isolation first can warm up joints for heavy compounds\n\nBest for intermediate lifters who want a sustainable, high-effort full-body split without rigid exercise prescriptions.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":90,"joins":7,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-15T19:07:04.911Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["A","B","A","B"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Leg Curl","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":9},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":10}]},{"n":"Pendulum Squat","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":10,"rpe":8},{"lo":8,"hi":10,"rpe":9}]},{"n":"Ben